                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b7e224bc-42c5-39f2-8ebd-a74c47f87fcd",
                        "entry_id": "18b57558-9661-3236-a143-1614bd1cdc54",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A heavy wind; a wind that brings a storm."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "6d6cd966-0353-4dcd-a5f3-f72f6ec03fd4",
                                "sense_id": "b7e224bc-42c5-39f2-8ebd-a74c47f87fcd",
                                "text": "Also know, that it is as possible to see the winde as the aire, their substances being too tenuous to be perceived; unlesse in a storm-winde, whose matter is an exhalation so thick that it darkens the aire:"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "91213c03-7cab-44f5-b852-311a6e50c8bf",
                                "sense_id": "b7e224bc-42c5-39f2-8ebd-a74c47f87fcd",
                                "text": "1839, Henry Wadsworth Longfellow, Hyperion, New York: Samuel Colman, Volume 1, Book 2, Chapter 6, p. 146,\n[…] the stormwind smites the wall of the mountain cliff […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d77f7a64-f6bd-4c66-b329-e46703830cf7",
                                "sense_id": "b7e224bc-42c5-39f2-8ebd-a74c47f87fcd",
                                "text": "1872, Lewis Carroll, Through the Looking-Glass, London: Macmillan, prefatory poem,\nWithout, the frost, the blinding snow,\nThe storm-wind’s moody madness—\nWithin, the firelight’s ruddy glow,\nAnd childhood’s nest of gladness."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6544e146-fee4-413c-a03a-950acc7f0d7e",
                                "sense_id": "b7e224bc-42c5-39f2-8ebd-a74c47f87fcd",
                                "text": "That spell of sea-safety which they set much store by in the Northern Archipelago never saved a man from storm-wind or storm-wave, but, cast by one who knows the local seas and the ways of a boat and the skills of the sailor, it weaves some daily safety about the fisherman."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "cba69181-1c20-4cb0-9aee-6441c21edf3a",
                                "sense_id": "b7e224bc-42c5-39f2-8ebd-a74c47f87fcd",
                                "text": "He slashed right and left as the fallen banner was ripped apart, the thousand ragged pieces swirling away like crimson leaves in a stormwind."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
